DOCUMENT 3 — HESS V3 REBUILD & IMPLEMENTATION SPECIFICATION

Status: Active Development Specification
Version: V3
Purpose: Define how Hess should be rebuilt from the existing repository into a coherent, testable, expandable game.


---

1. REBUILD PHILOSOPHY

Hess should not be treated as a normal chess application with several unusual pieces added to it.

Hess is its own board game.

The rebuild therefore begins from the question:

> “What implementation best represents the Hess rules and experience?”



rather than:

> “How do we repair the existing implementation?”



The existing repository remains valuable as:

a source of already-developed mechanics,

a source of working UI ideas,

a source of game logs,

a source of AI implementation,

a source of discovered bugs,

and evidence of how previous implementations interpreted the rules.


However, existing code should not automatically be considered authoritative.

The new implementation must establish a single coherent source of truth.


---

2. AUTHORITATIVE SYSTEM STRUCTURE

The rebuilt game should have a clear separation between:

A. Rules Engine

Responsible for determining:

legal moves,

captures,

movement restrictions,

checks,

King Swaps,

Jester states,

Queen restrictions and raids,

promotion/transformation,

sacrifice mechanics,

game-ending conditions,

turn ownership,

special-state transitions.


The rules engine must not depend on the UI.


---

B. Game State

Represents the complete state of a Hess match.

This includes more than piece positions.

The state may contain:

board position,

active player,

piece identities,

piece types,

piece movement history where relevant,

pawn starting-state information,

King Swap availability,

Jester direction/state,

Queen raid state,

sacrifice state,

hidden setup state,

move history,

repetition information,

two-King countdown,

game status,

pending player decisions.


The engine must be capable of reconstructing a position from this state without relying on UI state.


---

C. Move / Action System

A Hess action is not necessarily equivalent to a conventional chess move.

The system should support actions such as:

normal movement,

capture,

King Swap,

Jester-related decisions,

sacrifice selection,

transformation selection,

special-state continuation.


A move/action should contain enough information for the game to:

1. validate it,


2. apply it,


3. reverse it where necessary for AI search/testing,


4. record it,


5. reproduce the resulting state.




---

D. UI

The UI should display the state supplied by the game system.

The UI must not independently decide:

> “This looks like a legal move.”



Instead:

> Engine → legal actions → UI presentation



This prevents the previous frontend/server rules divergence.


---

E. AI

The AI must consume the same authoritative game representation used by human play.

It must not maintain a simplified version of Hess rules merely because conventional chess search is easier.


---

3. RULES ARE CURRENTLY STABLE, NOT FROZEN

The current Hess rules should be treated as:

IMPLEMENTABLE + REVISION-READY

rather than:

PERMANENTLY FINAL

This distinction is important.

Hess is still young, and actual play may expose:

unintended exploits,

unclear interactions,

degenerate strategies,

confusing mechanics,

impossible states,

interesting new possibilities,

or rules that simply feel wrong once many people play them.


Therefore the architecture must make rule changes relatively cheap.

Avoid scattering numerical values and special-case assumptions throughout the application.

Examples:

Queen raid length should be a rule configuration.

Two-King countdown should be a rule configuration.

King Swap limits should be represented explicitly.

Piece movement definitions should be centralized.

Jester states should be explicit rather than inferred from UI behavior.



---

4. UNRESOLVED RULES

Some Hess interactions are not sufficiently settled yet.

They must not be silently invented and presented as official rules.

Instead, use the following structure:

RULE STATUS

CONFIRMED
Currently accepted Hess rule.

PROVISIONAL
Temporary implementation rule used until testing resolves it.

UNRESOLVED
Known ambiguity requiring further design/testing.

DISCOVERED EDGE CASE
A behavior exposed through actual play that requires investigation.

EXPERIMENTAL
A deliberately testable variation.

This allows development to continue without pretending every unanswered question has already been solved.


---

5. TEMPORARY RULE PRINCIPLE

When an unresolved interaction prevents the game from functioning, the implementation should choose the least surprising, most internally consistent temporary rule.

The temporary rule must be:

1. deterministic,


2. documented,


3. isolated,


4. easy to change,


5. visible to developers/testers.



The engine must never produce:

> “I don't know what happens here, so freeze.”



A Hess position should always resolve into one of:

legal continuation,

player decision,

game-ending result,

explicitly defined unresolved/provisional behavior.



---

6. PIECE SYSTEM

Every Hess piece should have an explicit rules definition.

At minimum:

identity,

type,

owner,

position,

movement generator,

capture behavior,

special interactions,

restrictions,

state requirements.


The system should not assume that piece strength or importance follows conventional chess hierarchy.

For example:

> A pawn's strategic importance may change dramatically depending on its position and the possibility of a King Swap.



Likewise:

a Jester may be strategically valuable because of its lifecycle,

a Queen's normal movement is constrained by its goalkeeper role,

the Missile Rook's jumping geometry produces unusual tactical relationships,

the Staircase Bishop's movement creates nontraditional attack patterns,

the T-Shape Knight's movement creates positional possibilities unlike a normal Knight.


The AI must understand these relationships rather than assigning every piece a mostly static chess-like value.


---

7. MOVEMENT ENGINE

Movement generation must be coordinate-based.

Do not rely on fragile board-array arithmetic such as:

index ± 4
index ± 6

unless the operation is explicitly safe for the relevant geometry.

Every movement rule should conceptually operate on:

(row, column)

and then convert the result into a board position.

This prevents:

wraparound,

accidental diagonals,

edge-of-board errors,

false legal moves,

incorrect distance calculations.



---

8. PAWN STATE

Pawn movement must explicitly track whether a pawn is eligible for its initial double-step.

This must not simply be inferred from:

> “The pawn happens to be standing on its original rank.”



This is especially important because Hess permits unusual interactions such as King Swap.

Example edge case:

> A King swaps positions with a pawn that has never moved.



The engine must distinguish:

Pawn has never moved

from:

Pawn happens to occupy a starting-rank square.

The correct behavior for the double-step must therefore be determined from pawn state, not merely coordinate.

This is also an important test case because previous gameplay exposed suspicious double-move behavior.


---

9. MIDFIELD RULES

The midfield line must be treated as a piece-specific spatial restriction, not as a universal movement barrier.

The existence of the midfield line must not accidentally alter ordinary movement for pieces that are permitted to cross it.

For example:

a Queen may be restricted from crossing it during normal play,

a Queen Raid may temporarily override that restriction,

other pieces may cross it according to their normal movement rules.


The engine therefore must never implement:

> “Pieces cannot cross the midfield.”



unless a particular piece's rules explicitly require that.


---

10. KING SWAP

King Swap must be implemented as a proper game action.

It is not simply:

King.position = Pawn.position
Pawn.position = King.position

The resulting state must preserve all relevant information.

In particular, the engine must correctly handle:

pawn movement history,

resulting attacks,

resulting check state,

legality of the swap,

remaining swaps,

turn continuation,

edge-of-board positions,

situations where the swap creates or escapes threats.


King Swap must be tested from unusual starting arrangements, including pieces placed close to board edges.


---

11. JESTER SYSTEM

The Jester should be implemented as a stateful piece rather than a simple movement type.

Its state may include concepts such as:

heading home
at home
returning
returning pawn

The exact internal names are implementation details.

The important requirement is that the engine knows where the Jester is in its lifecycle.

The Jester must correctly support:

promotion into Jester,

movement toward home,

home-rank decision,

continued Jester status,

direction reversal where applicable,

returning-pawn behavior,

interaction with enemy attacks,

bounce/deflection,

sacrifice decision,

edge cases involving unavailable sacrifices.



---

12. JESTER SACRIFICE SAFETY

The previous implementation exposed a critical failure:

> JESTER_SACRIFICE can become impossible to resolve when the owner has no eligible sacrifice.



The rebuilt engine must treat this as a transition problem, not a UI problem.

Whenever a sacrifice decision occurs:

1. Determine whether a legal sacrifice exists.


2. If yes → enter player decision state.


3. If no → resolve according to the defined rule.


4. Never leave the game waiting for an impossible choice.



The UI must therefore never display:

> “Choose a piece”



when there are zero selectable pieces.


---

13. SPECIAL ACTION STATES

Hess should support explicit pending decisions.

Examples:

NORMAL_TURN
KING_SWAP_DECISION
JESTER_SACRIFICE
JESTER_HOME_DECISION
QUEEN_RAID
GAME_OVER

The exact state names may change.

The architectural principle should not.

A special rule that requires player choice must become a real game state.

This makes the system:

easier to test,

easier for the AI to reason about,

easier to reconnect,

easier to render,

harder to accidentally soft-lock.



---

14. AI IS A CORE COMPONENT

The Hess AI should not be treated as:

> “Chess AI + custom movement.”



That approach is likely to produce an AI that technically plays legal moves while fundamentally misunderstanding Hess.

The AI must understand that Hess has a much larger decision space.

A position may require deciding:

which piece to attack,

whether attacking a Jester is actually beneficial,

whether sacrificing a piece is better than preserving material,

whether a pawn is strategically dangerous because of King Swap,

whether to expose a King to enable a future swap,

whether to use a special mechanic now or preserve it,

whether a Queen should remain restricted,

whether entering a Queen Raid is beneficial,

whether a Jester should transform,

whether maintaining a strange position creates future tactical leverage.



---

15. AI EVALUATION

The evaluation function should not rely primarily on traditional material values.

Instead, Hess evaluation should eventually consider categories such as:

Material

What pieces remain?

King safety

How vulnerable is the King?

Mobility

How many useful actions are available?

Tactical threats

What immediate captures or forced sequences exist?

Pawn potential

How dangerous are advanced or strategically positioned pawns?

Swap potential

How valuable are available King Swap opportunities?

Jester potential

What is the Jester's current lifecycle state and future value?

Queen potential

How close is the position to enabling a Queen Raid, and what is its tactical value?

Position

Does the current geometry create advantageous movement relationships?

Future options

How many meaningful choices does the position preserve?

Forced consequences

Does a move create an unavoidable special interaction?

The evaluation system should be extensible rather than one giant hard-coded formula.


---

16. AI SEARCH CORRECTNESS

The search algorithm must understand that:

> A Hess action does not necessarily mean “switch player.”



A Jester bounce/deflection can result in the same player retaining the turn.

Therefore the search tree must derive the next actor from the resulting game state, not blindly alternate:

White → Black → White → Black

This is a fundamental requirement.


---

17. AI REPETITION

Hess contains enough unusual movement and defensive possibilities that repetition can occur naturally.

The AI should therefore understand repetition at two levels:

Rule-level repetition

If Hess eventually defines a formal repetition rule, the engine must enforce it.

Search-level repetition

During AI evaluation, repetitive sequences should be recognized so the AI does not waste search depth repeatedly exploring meaningless cycles.

A penalty may be used during evaluation, but it must not replace the actual Hess game rule if a formal repetition rule exists.


---

18. AI SELF-PLAY

Self-play should become a major testing mechanism.

The system should eventually support large numbers of games generated without human intervention.

Self-play can expose:

illegal move generation,

loops,

impossible states,

dominant strategies,

useless pieces,

broken mechanics,

AI exploits,

evaluation failures,

unexpectedly powerful pawn positions,

Jester abuse,

King Swap abuse.


However:

> Self-play results are evidence, not proof that the rules are correct.



A million games of a broken engine can simply produce a million examples of the same broken assumption.


---

19. AI TRAINING ARCHITECTURE

AI training and AI gameplay should be separated.

Gameplay AI

Responsible for choosing moves quickly.

Training system

Responsible for:

self-play,

replay generation,

evaluation experiments,

parameter updates,

model/weight persistence.


Training must not block active multiplayer games.

Long-running operations should be isolated from the live game loop.


---

20. AI DATA SAFETY

Training data and weights must use:

explicit paths,

error reporting,

atomic writes,

recovery-safe loading,

version information.


A failed write must never silently appear successful.

A corrupted AI file should not silently become the new active model.


---

21. TESTING STRATEGY

Hess requires more than traditional unit testing.

Testing should exist at several levels.


---

LEVEL 1 — Rule Unit Tests

Test individual rules.

Examples:

Missile Rook movement,

Staircase Bishop movement,

T-Shape Knight restrictions,

Pawn double-step,

Queen midfield restriction,

Jester movement,

King movement.



---

LEVEL 2 — Interaction Tests

Test combinations.

Examples:

Pawn + King Swap,

King Swap + check,

King Swap + pawn movement state,

Jester + capture,

Jester + sacrifice,

Jester + no sacrifice,

Jester + home transformation,

Queen + midfield,

Queen Raid + capture,

two Kings + countdown.



---

LEVEL 3 — Position Tests

Construct deliberately strange positions.

Examples:

King on the edge,

Queen on unusual ranks,

pieces surrounding a King,

minimal-material positions,

multiple advanced pawns,

Jester near both ends,

Kings in unusual relative positions.



---

LEVEL 4 — Full Game Tests

Play complete games from:

normal setup,

unusual setup,

aggressive setup,

defensive setup,

intentionally strange setup.



---

LEVEL 5 — Human Playtesting

Humans should deliberately attempt to break the game.

The tester should ask:

> “What happens if I do something weird?”



rather than merely:

> “Does the documented example work?”




---

LEVEL 6 — AI-vs-AI Testing

Allow the AI to generate thousands of games and inspect:

freezes,

repetitions,

illegal states,

unexpected dominant moves,

suspicious mechanics,

game length,

piece survival rates,

terminal conditions.



---

22. THE EDGE-CASE MINDSET

Hess testing cannot be reduced to:

> “Check every rule once.”



The space of possible positions is enormous.

A legal rule can still produce an unexpected interaction with another legal rule.

Therefore testing should intentionally explore:

Rule A
+
Rule B
+
Unusual position
+
Unusual setup
+
Boundary square
+
Previous movement history

This is particularly important for Hess because hidden setup and unusual piece mechanics can produce positions that conventional chess testing would never encounter.


---

23. GAME LOGS AS EVIDENCE

Saved human/AI games should be preserved.

They can be used to investigate:

actual player behavior,

unexpected tactics,

AI mistakes,

repeated positions,

suspicious pawn movement,

Jester interactions,

King Swap usage,

unusual endgames.


However, existing logs should not be treated as the complete test suite.

They represent only a tiny sample of the possible Hess state space.


---

24. MOVE LOGGING

The rebuilt game should produce machine-readable move history.

Each action should ideally preserve:

player,

action type,

starting position,

destination,

captured piece if applicable,

special-state transition,

decisions made,

resulting state information where useful.


Human-readable notation can then be generated from this underlying action data.

This prevents the notation system from becoming the actual game-state system.


---

25. REPLAYABILITY

A completed game should eventually be replayable.

A replay should reconstruct the game through the authoritative engine rather than simply moving visual pieces around.

This creates another powerful debugging tool:

> If a saved game cannot be replayed deterministically, something is wrong.




---

26. MULTIPLAYER ARCHITECTURE

The server must remain authoritative.

Client:

> “I want to perform this action.”



Server/engine:

> “Is that action legal?”



If legal:

> Apply action → produce new state → broadcast appropriate information.



The client must never be trusted to declare:

> “This move is legal.”




---

27. HIDDEN SETUP

During setup:

each player's arrangement remains private,

individual swaps must not leak,

the opponent should receive only the information necessary to know that setup is progressing,

complete arrangements become visible only at the appropriate reveal point.


This should be enforced server-side.

It must not depend merely on hiding elements in the UI.


---

28. SECURITY BASELINE

Before public deployment:

reconnect credentials must not leak,

admin functionality must not use unsafe default secrets,

socket payloads must be validated,

room access must be protected,

CORS must be restricted appropriately,

privileged AI controls must be authenticated,

reconnect credentials should have sensible lifecycle/security properties.


Security is part of the rebuild, not a later polish pass.


---

29. UI/UX IMPLEMENTATION PRINCIPLE

The rebuilt interface should communicate that Hess is:

familiar enough to approach, strange enough to discover.

It should not look like a generic chess clone.

The UI should emphasize:
board clarity,
piece identity,
legal actions,
special-state feedback,
player decisions,
move history,
discovery.
Complexity should appear when relevant, rather than permanently occupying the screen.
30. RULE LEARNING SYSTEM
The rebuilt game should contain three complementary learning layers:
Basic
Teach the fundamentals quickly.
Interactive
Allow players to experiment with pieces in the Piece Academy.
Contextual
Explain unusual mechanics precisely when they occur during an actual game.
This prevents Hess from becoming a giant rulebook that players must memorize before playing.
31. PIECE ACADEMY
The Piece Academy should allow players to select a piece and experiment with it.
The academy should demonstrate:
movement,
boundaries,
captures,
blockers,
special movement restrictions.
It should eventually also provide an interaction mode where pieces can interact with other pieces.
This is important because understanding:
“How does this piece move?”
is only the first layer of understanding Hess.
The more interesting question becomes:
“What happens when this piece meets another piece?”
32. INTERACTION ACADEMY
The interaction system should eventually cover combinations such as:
Pawn ↔ King Swap,
Jester ↔ attacking piece,
Jester ↔ sacrifice,
Queen ↔ midfield,
Queen ↔ Raid,
pieces ↔ blocking,
pieces ↔ capture,
special-state interactions.
These should be introduced progressively rather than dumping every interaction onto the player at once.
33. GUIDED MATCH
A first Hess match should allow the player to actually play.
The AI should behave as an Easy teaching opponent, not as a scripted puppet.
When a rare mechanic becomes relevant for the first time:
Pause.
Explain.
Highlight the relevant action.
Allow the player to perform it.
Resume.
The tutorial should teach through the actual position.
34. AI DIFFICULTY
Difficulty should eventually represent more than:
search depth.
Different levels should affect things such as:
search depth,
evaluation sophistication,
tactical awareness,
special-mechanic awareness,
planning horizon,
willingness to sacrifice,
positional understanding.
The hardest AI should not merely calculate more conventional chess moves.
It should understand more Hess.
35. REBUILD ORDER
The rebuild should follow this broad dependency order:
1. Establish Hess rules model
          ↓
2. Establish authoritative game state
          ↓
3. Implement unified rules engine
          ↓
4. Implement special-state transitions
          ↓
5. Build deterministic tests
          ↓
6. Build human game UI
          ↓
7. Build multiplayer synchronization
          ↓
8. Build Piece Academy
          ↓
9. Integrate Hess AI
          ↓
10. AI-vs-AI testing
          ↓
11. Human playtesting
          ↓
12. Edge-case discovery
          ↓
13. Rule refinement
          ↓
14. Security/deployment hardening
This order is intentional.
The AI should not be optimized around an unstable rules engine.
The UI should not contain its own rules implementation.
Multiplayer should not be used to hide engine problems.
36. DEFINITION OF “DONE”
Hess V3 should not be considered complete merely because:
the board renders,
pieces move,
multiplayer works,
AI produces moves,
and games can finish.
A meaningful V3 completion requires:
Rules
The documented rules are consistently represented by one authoritative engine.
Stability
Known freezes and deadlocks are eliminated.
Correctness
Client and server cannot disagree about legal actions.
Testing
Core rules and important interactions have automated coverage.
AI
The AI understands Hess-specific mechanics and does not merely imitate conventional chess evaluation.
UX
Players can learn Hess without reading the entire ruleset beforehand.
Multiplayer
Hidden setup and private information remain private.
Security
Room credentials and administrative functionality are appropriately protected.
Discoverability
Players can understand unfamiliar mechanics through the Academy and contextual explanations.
Extensibility
New Hess rules can be introduced without rewriting the entire application.
37. THE V3 DEVELOPMENT RULE
The most important rule for development is:
Do not preserve broken architecture merely because it already exists.
If an existing component is good:
reuse it.
If an existing component contains useful logic:
extract it.
If an existing component conflicts with the new architecture:
replace it.
If an old implementation makes a rule work only through a strange workaround:
investigate the rule and rebuild the behavior properly.
The objective is not to preserve the old Hess implementation.
The objective is to build the best current implementation of Hess.
38. FINAL DEVELOPMENT POSITION
Hess V3 should be treated as a new generation of the game, not simply an update patch.
The old repository answers:
“What have we already built?”
The Hess V3 documents answer:
“What are we trying to build now?”
Actual games answer:
“What happens when people really play it?”
Testing answers:
“Does the implementation behave correctly?”
And future playtesting answers:
“Are the rules themselves good enough?”
That distinction should remain throughout development.
End of Document 3 — HESS V3 Rebuild & Implementation Specification
This is the one I’d put in the repo as something like V3_REBUILD_SPEC.md. It deliberately doesn't pretend that every unresolved Hess rule has magically been settled. That would actually make the rebuild worse.
And honestly, this is the point where Hess starts looking less like “Tonbi made a chess variant” and more like “Tonbi accidentally designed an entire little strategy game and now has to build the damn thing properly.” 😂
